n: 4.1.8
name: keep
kind: hub
is: Carry forward: C as a strict small subset in one translation unit under about 3,000 lines; a table of host doors with C-ABI signatures as the whole public surface; MAP_JIT, a per-thread write toggle and one reserved region from the first line; a line-count gate on the C file; replacement of the C one function at a time until the system writes its own signed image; and the conditions under which the choice of C flips.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-bootstrap-language

n: 4.1.8.1
name: c-as-strict-small-subset
kind: finding
is: The bootstrap is written in a strict C99/C11 subset: one translation unit, a target under about 3,000 lines, no libc use beyond a declared list of OS symbols. The reason is not C's speed: the platform C ABI is the contract that libSystem, objc_msgSend, Vulkan, Win32 and Apple's JIT calls already speak, and a C bootstrap is that contract alone.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 4.1.1.2 4.1.1.3 4.1.1.6 4.1.1.8 4.1.2.3 4.1.3.1 4.1.4.1 4.1.4.2 4.1.4.7
limit: The 3,000-line target is the study's own figure; the critic records that two other first-round studies name smaller seeds. That the unit also builds with TinyCC and with zig cc is proposed as the test of the subset and was not run. That Vulkan speaks the platform C ABI is assumed; no Vulkan page was read.
see: 2.11.2#338-342
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-bootstrap-language

n: 4.1.8.2
name: host-door-table
kind: finding
is: The bootstrap's public surface is one table of host doors, entry points with C-ABI signatures: map an executable region, begin and end write for this thread, flush the instruction cache, map a file, load a library, find a symbol, raw call with N words, create a thread, clock, exit. Emitted code receives the table pointer and calls nothing else.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 4.1.1.6 4.1.1.7 4.1.1.8 4.1.4.2
limit: No such table was built or run in the study.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-bootstrap-language

n: 4.1.8.3
name: jit-contract-from-first-line
kind: finding
is: From its first line the bootstrap reserves one large MAP_JIT region once, writes only inside the per-thread toggle, and flushes the instruction cache after each write. One binary is then correct without the hardened runtime and, given the allow-jit entitlement, with it. A locally built developer binary needs no entitlement and no Apple account.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 4.1.1.1 4.1.1.3 4.1.1.4 4.1.1.6
limit: Under the hardened runtime without allow-jit the measured MAP_JIT mmap failed, so the study's phrase 'working in all signing states' overstates its own table. The toggle does not exist on iOS.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-bootstrap-language

n: 4.1.8.4
name: line-count-gate
kind: finding
is: A gate reads the line count of the C file against a hard budget. C gives no memory safety and nothing in the language stops accretion: the old seed reached 34,149 lines. What keeps the bootstrap small is the budget, together with the plan that the C shrinks as emitted code replaces it.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 4.1.4.4
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-bootstrap-language

n: 4.1.8.5
name: replace-one-function-at-a-time
kind: finding
is: A small evaluator in the bootstrap runs the one grammar's own code generator once. After that, emitted machine code replaces one C-ABI function at a time, until the system writes a signed Mach-O, ELF or PE image that holds its own host doors. The C file then remains as the start from nothing but a C compiler, and the emitted image is the hex seed.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 4.1.1.7 4.1.2.6 4.1.4.5 4.1.4.7 4.1.4.8
limit: Not built. The study offers a copy-and-patch tier in place of the evaluator; CPython's needs LLVM and Clang at build time, which the critic notes conflicts with a minimal stored bootstrap.
see: 2.11.2#338-342
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-bootstrap-language

n: 4.1.8.6
name: flip-conditions
kind: finding
is: The choice of C flips to Rust if the bootstrap stays above about 10,000 lines for years and parses untrusted input, or is to hold a ready-made code generator such as Cranelift; to Go for a hosted prototype whose emitted code is rare, short and never calls back; to a hex0 seed on Linux if audit from a few hundred bytes comes first.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 4.1.2.1 4.1.2.4 4.1.3.1 4.1.3.3 4.1.4.8 4.1.5.1
limit: The study's table has four more rows. Go is re-evaluated if its runtime supports foreign JIT frames, Zig if it reaches 1.0 with a native aarch64 backend. Two rows keep C: if Apple removes the toggle for apps without the allowlist, emission runs inside a registered callback; if iOS is a target, only interpretation or ahead-of-time images remain.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-bootstrap-language

n: 4.1.9
name: drop
kind: hub
is: Let go: Go as the bootstrap, because its runtime stays in the process and emitted code cannot replace it piecewise; Rust for a few thousand lines meant to dissolve, with Cranelift as a dependency; Zig as the language while it is pre-1.0; a hand-written hex or assembly seed as the starting point; and publishing code by write-then-mprotect, plain RWX mappings, or any thread patching any code.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-bootstrap-language

n: 4.1.9.1
name: go-as-bootstrap
kind: finding
is: Go is not the bootstrap language. Its runtime owns signals, stacks and the stop of all goroutines and cannot be replaced one function at a time by emitted code. A kernel that is normally inside JIT code would stall every collection or make the Go runtime part of the emitted code's calling convention.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 4.1.2.1 4.1.2.2 4.1.2.3 4.1.2.4 4.1.2.5 4.1.2.6
limit: Go stays a fit language for a host program; NUMS.Go is a Go workspace, of which the study read the directory listing only. The measured test binary, runtime included, is 1.6 MB stripped and 2.4 MB unstripped; the runtime's own share of it was not measured.
see: 2.2
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-bootstrap-language

n: 4.1.9.2
name: rust-as-bootstrap
kind: finding
is: Rust is not the bootstrap language for a few thousand lines meant to dissolve. Its runtime control equals C's; its safety covers only the non-JIT part: writing and calling machine code is unsafe in any language. Wasmtime's no_std build omits Cranelift and Winch, which need an OS that makes executable pages: a dependency the system would not keep.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 4.1.3.1 4.1.3.2 4.1.3.3
limit: That Rust's own ABI is unspecified is from model memory; only extern "C" would cross. Six-week releases and a 406 MB toolchain are the standing cost the study names.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-bootstrap-language

n: 4.1.9.3
name: zig-as-language
kind: finding
is: Zig is not the bootstrap language while it is pre-1.0: a language whose 0.17.0 release removed features and changed meaning without a compile error does not suit a file that must keep building for years. zig cc remains an optional tool for cross-linking the C bootstrap for Linux and Windows from the Mac.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 4.1.5.1
limit: Zig is not installed on the study machine; the reading rests on its pages alone. Only the 0.17.0 release notes were read, so whether every release removes features is not known.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-bootstrap-language

n: 4.1.9.4
name: hex-seed-as-start
kind: finding
is: A hand-written hex or assembly seed is not the starting point. It is proven on Linux, where a static binary may issue system calls directly; on macOS it would have to be a complete signed, dynamically linked Mach-O image, thousands of opaque bytes for each architecture and operating system.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 4.1.4.8 4.1.5.2
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-bootstrap-language

n: 4.1.9.5
name: write-then-mprotect-publishing
kind: finding
is: Three ways of publishing machine code are dropped. Writing a read-write page and switching it to read-execute with mprotect is killed under the hardened runtime. A plain RWX mapping without MAP_JIT is refused even without the hardened runtime. A design in which any thread patches any code does not fit a write permission that is per thread.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 4.1.1.1 4.1.1.2 4.1.1.3 4.1.1.5
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-bootstrap-language
